Leptospirosis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
High fever/severe headache, Exquisite calf muscle tenderness, Conjunctival suffusion without exudate, Exposure to contaminated fresh water

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-023
Reason for Visit: Abrupt high fever, excruciating calf pain, and red eyes following a recent camping trip, requiring urgent assessment to rule out severe systemic sepsis or impending renal failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young adult returning from a rural trip with sudden, severe rigors, agonizing muscle pain preventing walking, and an inability to tolerate oral fluids. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute systemic bacteremia (Weil's disease progression) and severe dehydration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm freezing, my head is pounding, and my calf muscles hurt so much I can't even stand up."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced abruptly 48 hours ago with high fever, severe shaking chills, and an intense relentless headache. The patient reports severe, excruciating generalized myalgia, classically demonstrating exquisite, severe tenderness upon palpation of the calf and lumbar muscles. The partner noted distinctive severe conjunctival suffusion characterized by intense redness without significant purulent exudate yesterday. Patient also reports nausea, recurrent vomiting, and profound anorexia. Patient has a history of distinct recent recreational exposure to fresh water (swam in a stagnant lake on a farm) 10 days ago.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely unable to bear weight due to exquisite calf tenderness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with partner; rapid deterioration over the last 12 hours is highly concerning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 39.4 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Acutely toxic appearance, diaphoretic, wincing with any lower limb movement, striking bilateral red eyes.
Physical Exam: Eye exam reveals marked bilateral conjunctival suffusion without purulent discharge. Musculoskeletal exam reveals exquisite, severe tenderness upon gentle palpation of the bilateral gastrocnemius (calf) muscles and lumbar paraspinals. Abdomen is soft, with mild tender hepatomegaly. Skin reveals a transient, distinct erythematous maculopapular rash on the trunk. No clinical jaundice noted yet. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: High fever/severe headache, Exquisite calf muscle tenderness, Conjunctival suffusion without exudate, and Exposure to contaminated fresh water.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Leptospirosis (Acute Anicteric Phase) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Dengue Fever - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct prominence of calf tenderness and conjunctival suffusion, and the specific exposure history (stagnant farm water), though the severe myalgia/headache overlap significantly.

Viral Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of classic nuchal rigidity, and the specific peripheral muscle involvement.

Malaria - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific local farm water exposure rather than tropical travel, and the presence of conjunctival suffusion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of leptospirosis: abrupt high fever, severe calf myalgia, and conjunctival suffusion, temporally linked to a high-risk fresh water exposure. The presence of tachycardia, borderline hypotension, and an inability to tolerate oral fluids due to vomiting raises immediate concern for progression to severe Weil's disease (acute renal failure, massive haemorrhage). A direct, urgent clinical evaluation was strictly required to assess haemodynamic stability, prescribe immediate targeted antibiotics to halt progression, and rule out current clinical jaundice or bleeding diathesis. This presentation could not safely wait until the next in-hours period (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe empiric oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily immediately (if tolerating oral intake) to shorten the duration and reduce severity. Request urgent comprehensive blood work first thing tomorrow: FBC, U&Es (to check for acute kidney injury), LFTs, and Leptospira serology/PCR. Administer an antiemetic to facilitate oral antibiotic retention.
Positive Behavioral Change: By forcing down 100mL of oral rehydration solution immediately after the antiemetic takes effect, the patient will actively combat the severe volume depletion; this hydration will reward them with an immediate reduction in the pounding headache, reinforcing the critical need to maintain fluid intake.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or hospital review within 24 hours to review renal and liver function tests.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops yellowing of the skin/eyes (jaundice), completely stops passing urine for 8 hours, starts coughing up blood (haemoptysis), or notices any unusual bruising or bleeding.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on classic epidemiological and clinical signs; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Leptospirosis can be biphasic. The first phase (current) is flu-like. Without antibiotics, it can progress to a severe immune phase causing liver and kidney failure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The bacteria from the contaminated water can severely damage your kidneys and liver. If you stop producing urine, or if you start coughing up any blood whatsoever, you must call an ambulance immediately as this is a life-threatening complication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have contracted a bacterial infection from animal urine present in the lake water you swam in. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but it is critical to start them immediately to prevent the infection from spreading to your major organs.
Actionable Steps: Take the Doxycycline with a large glass of water to prevent esophageal burning. Monitor your urine output meticulously.

